WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.697
This guided practice is a debugging exercise.

00:00:02.697 --> 00:00:07.537
The query joins patients to admissions, lab results, and prescriptions.

00:00:07.537 --> 00:00:14.067
Then it reports three different numbers: the total number of joined rows, a naive admission count,

00:00:14.067 --> 00:00:16.042
and a distinct admission count.

00:00:16.042 --> 00:00:20.852
The naive admission count uses COUNT on the admission ID without DISTINCT.

00:00:20.852 --> 00:00:26.912
Because an admission row can be repeated across combinations with several lab and prescription rows,

00:00:26.912 --> 00:00:30.502
that count may be larger than the true number of admissions.

00:00:30.502 --> 00:00:37.082
The distinct count uses the admission ID as the event key and removes repeated appearances of the same

00:00:37.082 --> 00:00:38.912
admission inside the joined result.

00:00:38.912 --> 00:00:41.302
Compare the two values carefully.

00:00:41.302 --> 00:00:45.022
The important question is not simply which number is smaller.

00:00:45.022 --> 00:00:52.527
Explain why the naive count can be inflated even though no admission was duplicated in the original admissions

00:00:52.527 --> 00:00:56.237
table. The duplication is created by the join combinations.

00:00:56.237 --> 00:01:00.152
This is the kind of debugging you will do in real analytical work.

00:01:00.152 --> 00:01:06.504
When a metric looks too large, inspect the relationship structure before assuming the source data is wrong.
